+## Operating characteristics
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query point for analytical primitives, as they involve a fixed number of arithmetic operations and square roots [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$, as no grid or mesh data is stored for the primitive itself [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Analytical SDFs are computationally cheap and serve as foundational building blocks [S2, unofficial]. However, recursive SDFs are costly and significantly impact performance, even with low recursion levels [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Exact distance calculation, no tessellation artifacts, infinite resolution, easy composition via CSG [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to specific shapes; complex shapes require composition or noise perturbation; recursive structures can be slow [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Ideal for simple geometric primitives, fractals, and scenes requiring exact distance bounds for sphere tracing [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the geometry is highly complex and cannot be easily decomposed into primitives or composed via CSG, or when high-performance rendering of recursive structures is required without bounding box optimization [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) for primitives were popularized in computer graphics by Ian McEwan's work on ray marching with analytical primitives [S1, unofficial]. The use of exact distance functions for spheres, boxes, and tori allows for efficient sphere tracing, a technique where the ray step size is determined by the distance field value itself [S2, S14, unofficial]. The mathematical foundations for distance queries to natural quadrics (planes, spheres, cylinders, cones, tori) are well-established in computational geometry [S2, unofficial]. The application of SDFs to constructive solid geometry (CSG) via min/max operators was formalized in the context of ray marching, enabling the creation of complex scenes from simple primitives [S3, S4, S11, S16, unofficial]. Recursive SDFs and fractal structures leverage these primitives to create self-similar geometry [S1, unofficial]. The integration of SDFs into differentiable rendering pipelines, such as those in Mitsuba 3, utilizes grid-based representations with interpolation to maintain gradient continuity for optimization [S4].
+The concept of using analytical functions to define 3D geometry dates back to the early days of computer graphics. The specific application of Signed Distance Fields for ray marching (sphere tracing) was popularized by Inigo Quilez in his online articles and shaders [S2, S3, unofficial]. The use of SDFs for constructive solid geometry and fractal generation has been widely adopted in the demoscene and real-time rendering communities [S2, S3, unofficial]. The integration of SDFs into differentiable rendering pipelines, such as in Mitsuba 3, represents a more recent development in inverse rendering [S5].
 
